  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 153 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cyclonic northwest flow will continue as the region \nremains under the influence of an upper level low this weekend. An \nembedded shortwave will pass through the region, bringing more \nwidespread shower activity this afternoon into the evening. It's \nbeen a mix of rain and snow across the region today, with snow \nmostly confined to the high terrain and portions of the Northeast \nKingdom this afternoon and rain most other locations. Although most \nlocations are above freezing, temperatures may drop significantly \nwithin some of the showers, allowing for a rain/snow mix or even a \nbrief period of snow at lower elevations. The widespread shower \nactivity will end tonight, with lingering showers tapering off \nthrough the day tomorrow. Total snowfall accumulations this weekend \nlook to generally be in the 1-4 inch range for locations above 2000 \nft, with little to no accumulation expected at lower elevations. \nTemperatures will continue to remain on the cooler side this \nweekend, with daytime highs only climbing into the 30s to lower 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Unimpactful weather is expected all of the way through \nthe upcoming week into next weekend. Following one last seasonably \ncool day on Monday, temperatures should be near to around 10 degrees \nabove normal for the remainder of the period. In fact, our large \nscale pattern strongly favors above normal temperatures to continue \nfor much of the following week, as well. This week does not look \nparticularly unseasonable, however. There is only a weak signal for \nunusually high daytime temperatures on Wednesday and Thursday, \nparticularly in the Champlain and Lower Connecticut River valleys, \nahead of a likely cold front. Said front is currently favored to \ntrack through with little fanfare Friday morning; eventually PoPs \nwill probably increase when timing of this boundary becomes more \nclear; many ensemble and deterministic models show a period of some \nlight precipitation associated with the feature. This will be the \nprimary exception to a period of otherwise fair weather, with \nridging re-establishing itself at the start of next weekend.",
